1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,870
Listen.

2
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:09,030
Practice.

3
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:09,910
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,250
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:14,070 --> 00:00:15,640
Welcome to another deep dive.

6
00:00:16,980 --> 00:00:19,360
Today we're going to be talking all about business English.

7
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:20,930
Oh, yeah.

8
00:00:21,010 --> 00:00:28,460
You've sent me just a mountain of resources on these essential phrases, so it's clear that you're looking to really refine your communication skills.

9
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:29,300
Absolutely.

10
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:31,070
Now, this could be kind of a dry topic.

11
00:00:31,190 --> 00:00:32,070
Oh, yeah, for sure.

12
00:00:32,170 --> 00:00:40,800
I think that we can definitely extract some really valuable nuggets from all this information, and you can walk away with some practical tips that you use.

13
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:41,480
Absolutely.

14
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:41,790
Right.

15
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:45,990
I think people don't realize how much language matters in business.

16
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:47,030
Yeah.

17
00:00:47,050 --> 00:00:49,400
You know, we think about just getting our point across.

18
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:51,380
But but it's so much more than that.

19
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:51,760
Right.

20
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:55,530
It's really about building trust, demonstrating professionalism.

21
00:00:56,410 --> 00:00:58,790
And really, at the end of the day, you're influencing outcomes.

22
00:00:58,920 --> 00:00:59,430
Absolutely.

23
00:00:59,550 --> 00:01:06,110
It's easy to get lost in all these phrases and all these different sections without really understanding the why behind them.

24
00:01:06,130 --> 00:01:06,650
Yeah.

25
00:01:06,670 --> 00:01:10,700
And we've got sections here on everything from networking to negotiation.

26
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:14,780
So let's just start with those initial greetings and networking phrases.

27
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:18,650
And I'm curious, like those classic lines, you know, it's a pleasure to meet you.

28
00:01:19,010 --> 00:01:19,230
Right.

29
00:01:19,650 --> 00:01:23,210
Are those still effective or do they come across as a bit outdated?

30
00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:26,520
Well, I mean, they're classics for a reason, you know?

31
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:27,060
Okay.

32
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:41,770
And I think if you think about the psychology behind them, like a formal greeting, it does convey a sense of respect and professionalism right away, which is so important when you're making a first impression.

33
00:01:41,830 --> 00:01:42,750
Yeah.

34
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:47,300
But, you know, it's not enough to just have that memorized line.

35
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:47,680
Right.

36
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:52,190
So how do you make those initial interactions more genuine and more memorable?

37
00:01:52,330 --> 00:01:54,640
Well, it's about going beyond the script.

38
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:58,120
OK. You know, so instead of just saying I've heard great things about your work.

39
00:01:58,200 --> 00:01:58,400
Right.

40
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:00,270
Try connecting it to something specific.

41
00:02:00,470 --> 00:02:06,540
OK. You know, so you could say something like I was really impressed with your company's recent campaign on whatever it was.

42
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:10,560
What were some of the biggest challenges you faced in developing that?

43
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:11,450
That's a great tip.

44
00:02:11,590 --> 00:02:13,590
I love that because it shows you've done your homework.

45
00:02:13,810 --> 00:02:14,170
Yeah.

46
00:02:14,190 --> 00:02:15,280
You're genuinely interested.

47
00:02:15,310 --> 00:02:17,540
And I bet people are more likely to open up.

48
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:18,260
Oh, yeah.

49
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:19,780
When they feel like you're truly engaged.

50
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:20,500
Absolutely.

51
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:31,720
And, you know, when you ask those insightful questions, you're not only gathering information, but you're building rapport and really setting the stage for a much more meaningful connection.

52
00:02:31,870 --> 00:02:32,410
Absolutely.

53
00:02:32,430 --> 00:02:34,360
OK, so we've we've nailed the first impression.

54
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:43,650
Let's move on to email, because I know I'm guilty of this overusing phrases like I hope this email finds you well.

55
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:44,510
Right, right.

56
00:02:44,690 --> 00:02:45,700
Do those really matter?

57
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:52,470
You know, they might seem a little formulaic, but they do serve a purpose, especially with new contacts, you know.

58
00:02:52,870 --> 00:02:53,070
Yeah.

59
00:02:53,050 --> 00:03:01,120
That little touch of formality does help to establish a professional tone and, you know, convey respect.

60
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:04,630
It's kind of like setting the right tone for that whole conversation.

61
00:03:04,810 --> 00:03:07,470
Yeah, that's an interesting way to think about it.

62
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:14,060
But I can also see how they could sound a bit robotic, especially in like, you know, back and forth exchanges.

63
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:14,620
You're right.

64
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:15,680
It's all about context.

65
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:20,470
OK. You know, so once you've established that rapport, you can be a bit less formal.

66
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:21,750
Yeah.

67
00:03:21,870 --> 00:03:30,100
You could try variations like I hope you're having a productive week or even just jump straight into the content if it's a more casual exchange.

68
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:30,470
Right.

69
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:33,210
So it's like having different tools in your communication toolbox.

70
00:03:33,370 --> 00:03:33,850
Yeah.

71
00:03:33,990 --> 00:03:35,510
And choosing the right one for the job.

72
00:03:35,790 --> 00:03:36,350
Exactly.

73
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:37,300
How about meetings?

74
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:37,960
OK. Yeah.

75
00:03:37,940 --> 00:03:41,060
There's so many phrases in here for managing discussions.

76
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:47,010
Everything from let's get started to can we table this for now?

77
00:03:47,170 --> 00:03:47,450
Right.

78
00:03:47,610 --> 00:03:49,550
Like, do we really need all of these?

79
00:03:49,690 --> 00:03:53,300
Well, I think, you know, if you think about the purpose of those phrases.

80
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:53,920
Okay.

81
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:55,460
They're not just about being polite.

82
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:55,760
Right.

83
00:03:55,780 --> 00:03:58,750
They're really about keeping the meeting focused.

84
00:03:58,770 --> 00:03:58,870
Fine.

85
00:03:59,010 --> 00:04:00,770
Respectful of everyone's time.

86
00:04:00,790 --> 00:04:00,890
Yeah.

87
00:04:01,010 --> 00:04:02,510
And ultimately productive.

88
00:04:02,490 --> 00:04:03,010
Yeah.

89
00:04:03,030 --> 00:04:12,870
You know, even something simple like let's get started might seem obvious, but it signals that shift from informal chat to focused work.

90
00:04:13,390 --> 00:04:13,910
That's true.

91
00:04:14,470 --> 00:04:16,600
It sets the stage for a more productive discussion.

92
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:17,000
Yeah.

93
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:19,320
What about that phrase tabling a discussion?

94
00:04:19,530 --> 00:04:20,050
Oh, yeah.

95
00:04:20,450 --> 00:04:22,590
I've never been quite sure how to use that effectively.

96
00:04:22,570 --> 00:04:30,490
So tabling a topic is a great way to acknowledge a point that's important, but maybe not relevant to the immediate discussion.

97
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:35,910
So imagine a situation where someone brings up a valid point,

98
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:38,610
But it's going to totally derail the meeting.

99
00:04:38,630 --> 00:04:39,050
Yeah.

100
00:04:39,190 --> 00:04:40,750
If you dive into it right then.

101
00:04:40,810 --> 00:04:41,230
Yeah.

102
00:04:41,310 --> 00:04:45,300
You could say something like, that's a great point and I'd love to discuss it further.

103
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:45,800
Okay.

104
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:47,460
But perhaps we can table it for now.

105
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:48,240
Okay.

106
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:51,170
And schedule a separate meeting to explore it in detail.

107
00:04:51,350 --> 00:04:54,170
Oh, that's a much more diplomatic way to handle those situations.

108
00:04:54,370 --> 00:04:54,570
Right.

109
00:04:54,830 --> 00:04:56,000
Than I've used in the past.

110
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:56,360
Yeah.

111
00:04:56,840 --> 00:05:00,540
I could see how that would help keep things on track without dismissing anyone's ideas.

112
00:05:00,610 --> 00:05:01,250
Exactly.

113
00:05:01,330 --> 00:05:07,640
It shows respect for everyone's time and it ensures that the meeting stays focused on achieving those objectives.

114
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:08,460
Okay.

115
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:14,150
Let's move on to making requests because it seems like there's a whole spectrum of politeness here.

116
00:05:14,390 --> 00:05:14,750
Yeah.

117
00:05:14,830 --> 00:05:17,190
From a direct, could you please?

118
00:05:17,410 --> 00:05:17,670
Yeah.

119
00:05:17,730 --> 00:05:22,000
To a more tentative, would it be possible to?

120
00:05:22,220 --> 00:05:22,420
Right.

121
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:23,980
So when do you use which?

122
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:26,840
Well, I think there is a nuance to each phrase.

123
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:27,490
Okay.

124
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,230
And, you know, choosing the right one does depend on the context.

125
00:05:30,590 --> 00:05:30,690
Okay.

126
00:05:30,930 --> 00:05:34,940
Could you please is generally appropriate for straightforward requests.

127
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:35,620
Uh-huh.

128
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:37,790
Whereas would it be possible to... Yeah.

129
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:42,140
Might be better for larger or more complex asks.

130
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:42,600
Okay.

131
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:45,160
Especially when you're not sure if it's feasible.

132
00:05:45,330 --> 00:05:45,630
Right.

133
00:05:45,670 --> 00:05:49,480
So it's about striking that balance between directness and diplomacy.

134
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:49,900
Yeah.

135
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:51,380
Depending on the situation.

136
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:54,650
And I imagine the why behind the request is important, too.

137
00:05:54,790 --> 00:05:55,390
Absolutely.

138
00:05:55,410 --> 00:06:02,980
You know, instead of just saying, I need this report by tomorrow, try framing it in a way that highlights the benefits or the urgency.

139
00:06:03,460 --> 00:06:04,370
So you could say,

140
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:07,250
Would it be possible to have this report by tomorrow?

141
00:06:07,870 --> 00:06:11,670
It would really help me finalize the presentation for the client meeting on Friday.

142
00:06:11,690 --> 00:06:11,930
Right.

143
00:06:11,950 --> 00:06:17,400
So by explaining the reason behind the request, you're much more likely to get a positive response.

144
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:18,420
Absolutely.

145
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:21,360
It's about collaboration, not just barking orders.

146
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:22,220
Exactly.

147
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:24,820
Now, how about those dreaded feedback conversations?

148
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:25,430
Well, yeah.

149
00:06:25,450 --> 00:06:26,530
They can be so awkward.

150
00:06:26,730 --> 00:06:27,530
They can be tricky.

151
00:06:27,730 --> 00:06:31,570
Do these phrases, I appreciate your effort and here's a suggestion.

152
00:06:32,370 --> 00:06:32,510
Mm-hmm.

153
00:06:32,490 --> 00:06:34,000
Do they really make a difference?

154
00:06:34,120 --> 00:06:36,640
They can they can definitely make a big difference, you know.

155
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:37,120
OK.

156
00:06:37,320 --> 00:06:40,160
When you start with I appreciate your effort.

157
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:40,700
Yeah.

158
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:43,420
You're immediately acknowledging the person's work.

159
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:43,900
Right.

160
00:06:44,290 --> 00:06:48,450
And you're creating a more receptive environment for that constructive feedback.

161
00:06:48,470 --> 00:06:50,670
It's like you're taking the sting out of the criticism.

162
00:06:50,770 --> 00:06:51,290
Yeah.

163
00:06:51,310 --> 00:06:52,270
Before you even get there.

164
00:06:52,290 --> 00:06:52,830
Exactly.

165
00:06:52,850 --> 00:06:57,240
But I still struggle with like how to actually deliver the feedback itself.

166
00:06:57,260 --> 00:06:57,560
OK.

167
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:00,440
Sometimes it feels like I'm just picking apart someone's work.

168
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:02,300
Well, that's where specificity is key.

169
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:05,390
OK. You know, so instead of saying this could be better.

170
00:07:05,450 --> 00:07:05,970
Right.

171
00:07:06,290 --> 00:07:08,550
Try focusing on concrete examples.

172
00:07:09,350 --> 00:07:15,050
You could say in this section, I felt like the data could be presented more visually.

173
00:07:15,090 --> 00:07:19,150
Maybe a chart or graph would help to highlight the key trends.

174
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:22,610
Yeah, that's so much more helpful than just a vague suggestion.

175
00:07:22,670 --> 00:07:23,710
Exactly.

176
00:07:23,730 --> 00:07:25,640
And it shows you're paying attention to the details.

177
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:29,160
Yeah, and you always want to try to frame feedback as an opportunity for growth.

178
00:07:29,550 --> 00:07:30,030
Okay.

179
00:07:30,050 --> 00:07:32,830
So instead of focusing on the problem, focus on the solution.

180
00:07:32,850 --> 00:07:33,310
Okay.

181
00:07:33,330 --> 00:07:37,800
So you could say, I'm confident that with a few tweaks, this report will be even stronger.

182
00:07:37,780 --> 00:07:42,510
I like that it's about being supportive while still providing clear direction.

183
00:07:42,730 --> 00:07:42,950
Yeah.

184
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:43,940
Okay.

185
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:45,600
This has been really eye-opening.

186
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:45,960
Good.

187
00:07:46,260 --> 00:07:47,990
Let's shift gears to negotiation.

188
00:07:48,910 --> 00:07:49,370
All right.

189
00:07:49,390 --> 00:07:53,180
I think a lot of people feel uncomfortable with this aspect of business communication.

190
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:53,780
Yeah.

191
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:56,040
It can feel so adversarial.

192
00:07:56,380 --> 00:07:59,300
It's interesting how negotiation gets a bad rap.

193
00:07:59,540 --> 00:07:59,760
Yeah.

194
00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:01,170
It doesn't have to be a battle.

195
00:08:01,770 --> 00:08:04,910
It's really about finding a mutually beneficial solution.

196
00:08:05,070 --> 00:08:05,290
Great.

197
00:08:05,370 --> 00:08:07,690
And that requires a different mindset and language.

198
00:08:08,250 --> 00:08:14,080
So what are some phrases that can help us shift from that battle mentality to a more collaborative approach?

199
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:21,790
Well, you know, when you're presenting your side, try phrases like, here's why I believe this approach would be mutually beneficial.

200
00:08:22,490 --> 00:08:22,670
Okay.

201
00:08:22,690 --> 00:08:26,180
Or let me outline the advantages of the solution for both of us.

202
00:08:27,060 --> 00:08:32,000
So you're highlighting the shared value rather than just focusing on your own needs.

203
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:32,400
Right.

204
00:08:32,420 --> 00:08:35,490
It's like you're inviting the other person to problem solve with you.

205
00:08:35,770 --> 00:08:36,030
Yes.

206
00:08:36,330 --> 00:08:37,690
Rather than just trying to get your way.

207
00:08:38,010 --> 00:08:38,530
Exactly.

208
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:40,680
I bet that makes people more receptive to your ideas.

209
00:08:40,660 --> 00:08:41,340
Absolutely.

210
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:44,060
And when it comes to finding that middle ground.

211
00:08:44,260 --> 00:08:44,500
Yeah.

212
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:51,830
Phrase like we're open to exploring different options or let's see if we can find a solution that meets both of our needs.

213
00:08:52,050 --> 00:08:52,290
Right.

214
00:08:52,330 --> 00:08:57,520
Can help to maintain that collaborative spirit, even if there are disagreements along the way.

215
00:08:57,620 --> 00:08:58,160
That makes sense.

216
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:03,020
It's about signaling flexibility and a willingness to compromise.

217
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:03,570
Yeah.

218
00:09:03,590 --> 00:09:04,950
Without sacrificing your goals.

219
00:09:04,970 --> 00:09:05,790
Exactly.

220
00:09:05,770 --> 00:09:09,740
Now, one area I always find challenging is dealing with data and reports.

221
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:10,500
Okay.

222
00:09:10,590 --> 00:09:15,000
I tend to stumble over the numbers, and I struggle to make the information engaging.

223
00:09:15,150 --> 00:09:16,210
That's a common challenge.

224
00:09:16,430 --> 00:09:22,200
But the right language can help you to transform that dry data into a compelling narrative.

225
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:29,220
So instead of just reciting statistics, try framing them in a way that highlights their significance.

226
00:09:29,820 --> 00:09:36,110
You could say something like, what's interesting here is that the data shows a clear trend towards.

227
00:09:37,090 --> 00:09:39,710
So it's about providing context and interpretation.

228
00:09:39,810 --> 00:09:40,230
Exactly.

229
00:09:40,250 --> 00:09:42,000
You're not just throwing numbers at people.

230
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:45,160
You're helping them to understand what those numbers actually mean.

231
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:45,540
Right.

232
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:51,750
And remember, storytelling can be just as powerful with data as it is with any other type of information.

233
00:09:51,930 --> 00:09:56,560
So you could say if we look at this data over the past year, it tells a story.

234
00:09:56,580 --> 00:10:01,790
I love that it's about turning data into a story that people can connect with.

235
00:10:02,010 --> 00:10:02,470
Exactly.

236
00:10:02,490 --> 00:10:05,480
It's so much more effective than just a bunch of bullet points on a slide.

237
00:10:05,660 --> 00:10:06,140
It is.

238
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:14,310
Now, let's talk about something that I think is often overlooked, which are those seemingly small moments at the beginning and end of conversations.

239
00:10:14,730 --> 00:10:20,420
You know how we open and close interactions can have a huge impact on how our messages proceed.

240
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:20,760
You're right.

241
00:10:20,780 --> 00:10:22,220
We talked about those initial greetings.

242
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:31,300
But I'm curious about how we can make those closing moments more impactful because it's easy to just say thanks for your time and move on.

243
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:31,740
Right.

244
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:33,960
But I feel like there's a missed opportunity there.

245
00:10:33,940 --> 00:10:34,940
There definitely is.

246
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,430
Those final moments are your chance to reinforce key points.

247
00:10:40,190 --> 00:10:44,910
Ensure those clear next steps and leave that positive lasting impression.

248
00:10:45,470 --> 00:10:51,060
So instead of just ending abruptly, try something like, I appreciate the discussion today.

249
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:54,740
I'm confident that we're moving in the right direction with this project.

250
00:10:54,960 --> 00:10:58,180
It's almost like you're creating a sense of closure and momentum.

251
00:10:58,790 --> 00:10:59,770
It's not just an ending.

252
00:11:00,270 --> 00:11:02,530
It's a transition to that next step.

253
00:11:02,510 --> 00:11:03,230
Exactly.

254
00:11:03,410 --> 00:11:08,150
And if there are specific action items, be sure to reiterate them clearly.

255
00:11:08,170 --> 00:11:10,880
You can say, moving forward, I'll do whatever it is.

256
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:13,850
And we can circle back next week to discuss progress.

257
00:11:13,930 --> 00:11:14,230
Yeah.

258
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:19,720
It's about leaving the conversation with a clear sense of purpose and direction.

259
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:20,440
Exactly.

260
00:11:20,580 --> 00:11:22,090
Shows you're organized and reliable.

