845
00:35:56,980 --> 00:36:02,410
Walking into a room full of strangers and trying to strike up conversations, it's like, where do you even begin?

846
00:36:02,450 --> 00:36:05,010
It's definitely a skill that takes practice.

847
00:36:05,030 --> 00:36:08,320
Remember, everyone's there for the same reason, to connect with other people.

848
00:36:08,940 --> 00:36:10,360
Find some common ground.

849
00:36:10,530 --> 00:36:10,830
Right.

850
00:36:10,870 --> 00:36:11,490
Common ground.

851
00:36:11,970 --> 00:36:13,450
But how do you actually find that?

852
00:36:13,470 --> 00:36:15,790
Like, what are some good conversation starters?

853
00:36:16,270 --> 00:36:19,760
A genuine compliment or observation can be a great icebreaker.

854
00:36:20,280 --> 00:36:22,360
You could say something like, I love your jacket.

855
00:36:22,460 --> 00:36:23,180
Where did you get it?

856
00:36:23,500 --> 00:36:26,670
Or that was a really interesting point you made during the panel discussion.

857
00:36:27,430 --> 00:36:29,970
Simple things like that can often lead to a deeper conversation.

858
00:36:30,190 --> 00:36:32,260
It's like you're finding that initial spark, right?

859
00:36:32,340 --> 00:36:32,880
Exactly.

860
00:36:33,060 --> 00:36:36,080
And once you've got that spark, you've got to keep the conversation flowing.

861
00:36:36,280 --> 00:36:36,560
Yeah.

862
00:36:36,840 --> 00:36:38,020
Any tips on how to do that?

863
00:36:38,080 --> 00:36:39,820
Sometimes I just run out of things to say.

864
00:36:39,860 --> 00:36:41,920
Active listening is key here.

865
00:36:42,700 --> 00:36:47,510
Ask open-ended questions that show you're genuinely interested in what the other person has to say.

866
00:36:48,130 --> 00:36:52,360
Things like, tell me more about, or what's your experience with?

867
00:36:53,020 --> 00:36:55,700
So it's more about being curious than being witty.

868
00:36:55,750 --> 00:36:56,290
You got it.

869
00:36:57,030 --> 00:36:59,860
People love to talk about themselves and what they're passionate about.

870
00:37:00,480 --> 00:37:04,740
If you listen attentively and ask thoughtful questions, you're already building a connection.

871
00:37:04,900 --> 00:37:09,790
It's not about trying to impress them with your own stories, but about making them feel heard.

872
00:37:10,190 --> 00:37:10,670
I like that.

873
00:37:10,710 --> 00:37:11,630
It takes the pressure off.

874
00:37:11,970 --> 00:37:15,840
No, how do you politely escape when you need to move on and talk to other people?

875
00:37:15,890 --> 00:37:18,640
It feels rude to just cut someone off.

876
00:37:19,510 --> 00:37:20,820
You're right, you don't want to be abrupt.

877
00:37:21,600 --> 00:37:29,270
Try something like, it was a pleasure meeting you, I'd love to continue this conversation another time, or I need to grab a drink, but I really enjoyed chatting with you.

878
00:37:29,430 --> 00:37:30,040
Those are good.

879
00:37:30,100 --> 00:37:34,040
You're acknowledging the conversation, but also signaling that you need to move on.

880
00:37:35,220 --> 00:37:39,370
OK, so we've covered a lot of positive stuff, building rapport, making connections.

881
00:37:39,830 --> 00:37:46,500
But what about when things go wrong, like when there's a misunderstanding or you need to deliver bad news?

882
00:37:46,590 --> 00:37:48,370
Yeah, those situations can be tough.

883
00:37:48,470 --> 00:37:51,410
It feels like one wrong word can make things so much worse.

884
00:37:51,690 --> 00:37:52,680
Definitely.

885
00:37:52,660 --> 00:37:56,000
Communication is even more important when things are difficult.

886
00:37:56,820 --> 00:37:58,310
Clear and concise is key.

887
00:37:58,850 --> 00:38:00,590
Don't sugarcoat or beat around the bush.

888
00:38:00,710 --> 00:38:02,210
It just leads to more confusion.

889
00:38:02,610 --> 00:38:05,820
So honesty is the best policy, even when it's hard.

890
00:38:06,440 --> 00:38:09,250
But how do you deliver bad news without sounding like a jerk?

891
00:38:09,680 --> 00:38:10,220
Empathy.

892
00:38:11,040 --> 00:38:12,890
Acknowledge how the other person might be feeling.

893
00:38:13,090 --> 00:38:20,020
Phrases like, I understand this is disappointing news or I know this is not what you wanted here can make a big difference.

894
00:38:20,120 --> 00:38:22,480
So you're showing that you understand their perspective.

895
00:38:22,540 --> 00:38:22,780
Right.

896
00:38:22,840 --> 00:38:24,800
You're acknowledging the impact it has on them.

897
00:38:24,840 --> 00:38:31,130
And even when you're giving difficult feedback, try to focus on the situation, not the person.

898
00:38:31,150 --> 00:38:38,420
Instead of saying you did this wrong, try I've noticed that or this particular approach hasn't been as effective.

899
00:38:38,550 --> 00:38:42,130
It's about addressing the issue without attacking the person.

900
00:38:42,230 --> 00:38:42,690
Exactly.

901
00:38:43,110 --> 00:38:48,380
And when you're dealing with complaints or tough questions, staying calm and professional is super important.

902
00:38:48,460 --> 00:38:51,510
It's so easy to get defensive when you feel like you're being criticized.

903
00:38:51,830 --> 00:38:52,090
I know.

904
00:38:52,150 --> 00:38:53,190
It's a natural reaction.

905
00:38:53,730 --> 00:38:56,670
But take a breath and try to stay level-headed.

906
00:38:57,300 --> 00:39:03,490
Use phrases like, let me make sure I understand your concern correctly, or could you elaborate on what you mean by it?

907
00:39:03,670 --> 00:39:07,350
It shows you're actively listening and trying to understand their viewpoint.

908
00:39:07,510 --> 00:39:09,560
It takes the heat out of the situation, right?

909
00:39:09,540 --> 00:39:09,860
Right.

910
00:39:10,340 --> 00:39:13,060
And if you need a minute to collect your thoughts, don't be afraid to pause.

911
00:39:14,000 --> 00:39:19,990
Sometimes saying, let me think about that for a moment, is better than blurting out something you'll regret later.

912
00:39:20,650 --> 00:39:22,410
Sometimes silence IS Golden.

913
00:39:22,450 --> 00:39:27,940
Now, what about those times when you need to stand up for yourself or push back on something you don't agree with?

914
00:39:28,300 --> 00:39:29,600
That's where assertiveness comes in.

915
00:39:30,300 --> 00:39:35,730
You need to be able to express your needs and opinions clearly and confidently without being aggressive.

916
00:39:36,420 --> 00:39:39,970
It's like finding that sweet spot between being strong and being respectful.

917
00:39:40,210 --> 00:39:40,390
Right.

918
00:39:40,830 --> 00:39:43,130
One thing that can really help is using I statements.

919
00:39:43,870 --> 00:39:48,840
Instead of saying you're wrong, try I see things differently or I have a different perspective on this.

920
00:39:49,860 --> 00:39:52,810
It takes a focus off of blaming and just states your own view.

921
00:39:53,110 --> 00:39:53,650
That's interesting.

922
00:39:53,690 --> 00:39:55,590
It feels less confrontational.

923
00:39:55,620 --> 00:39:56,240
Exactly.

924
00:39:56,340 --> 00:39:59,110
It's about owning your perspective without dismissing theirs.

925
00:39:59,670 --> 00:40:05,070
If you disagree with a decision, you can say, while I respect that decision, I have some concerns.

926
00:40:05,810 --> 00:40:07,860
Or, I'm not sure that's the best approach.

927
00:40:08,360 --> 00:40:10,240
It's direct but not disrespectful.

928
00:40:10,620 --> 00:40:11,020
Exactly.

929
00:40:11,480 --> 00:40:12,560
And then there's negotiation.

930
00:40:12,900 --> 00:40:19,690
Whether it's about salary, deadlines, or contract terms, clear communication is super important.

931
00:40:19,710 --> 00:40:22,170
Negotiation is a whole other ballgame.

932
00:40:22,270 --> 00:40:22,650
It is.

933
00:40:23,170 --> 00:40:27,110
One of the most useful phrases you can use in negotiation is, what would it take to?

934
00:40:27,740 --> 00:40:32,400
It opens up a dialogue and encourages the other person to think creatively about solutions.

935
00:40:33,040 --> 00:40:40,070
Like instead of saying, I need a higher salary, you could say, what would it take for me to reach a salary of desired amount?

936
00:40:40,270 --> 00:40:40,970
Oh, that's clever.

937
00:40:41,010 --> 00:40:42,460
You're not making demands.

938
